        {
          "id": "rich_surface_terpenes_trajectory",
          "description": "The rich column's surface layer at its FIRST chemistry call (step 1, 06 LST, T = 306.17 K, M = 2.3565e19 molec/cm3), where the terpenes are still at their initial values: API 1.2e10 and LIM 4.7e9 molec/cm3 against the 2.4e-3 they sit at on the idealized column. Alpha-pinene and limonene are consumed within the first hour of this run, so this is the one state that exercises reactions {{137:117}}..{{142:122}} at all -- and the aromatic and isoprene primaries (TOL 1.2e10, XYL 7.1e9, CRES 2.4e8, ISOP 4.7e10, ANOL 2.4e10) are at their largest here too. Their oxidation PRODUCTS have not been made yet, which is what the noon regime below is for.\n\nWhich column this is. The reference is the RICH chem_opt = 170 column, dumps/chem170_rich -- a SECOND reference run, not a recapture of the first. Same Weisman-Klemp sounding, same Kansas location, same 1997-06-20 12 UTC + 48 h, same namelist and the same binary as the four idealized regimes above; the only difference is ESM_RICH_IC=1, which raises seven initial mixing ratios that WRF-Chem's idealized gas profile leaves at its 1e-11 to 1e-13 ppb floor: TOL 0.50, XYL 0.30, CSL 0.01, ISO 2.00, API 0.50, LIM 0.20 and C2H5OH 1.00 ppb (chem/chemics_init.F, CBMZ_MOSAIC_KPP case, WRF fork branch earthsciml-instrumented-chem170). dumps/chem170_wrf is untouched and the four idealized regimes are unchanged. The two columns are NOT the same run at different times: their meteorology diverges too, because aerosol mass feeds Dudhia's scattering whatever aer_ra_feedback says (FORTRAN_BUGS N86).\n\nWhy it exists. On the idealized column the aromatic, isoprene and terpene branches of CBM-Z carry no flux at all -- twenty-one species sit below one molecule per cubic centimetre for the whole run, so a test on them asserts their absence. Here they are present and their reactions are exercised: 62 of the 64 integrated species exceed 100 molec/cm3 at this state, and the four that do not (O1D, N2O5 in daylight, and API and LIM once the first hour has consumed them) are genuinely small rather than absent.\n\nReference and tolerance. As for the idealized regimes, the reference is the CONTINUOUS mechanism: kernels/cbmz_driver sub-stepped with RCONST refreshed before each sub-step (FORTRAN_BUGS N107), here at RTOL 1e-9, ATOL 1e-9 and nsub = 10000. The tighter ATOL is what lets every species be pinned RELATIVELY, at rel 1e-5 with an absolute floor of 1e-9 molec/cm3 that binds on nothing -- there is no null test in this regime. The larger nsub is needed because THE FROZEN-COEFFICIENT SPLIT IS BIGGER ON THIS COLUMN: isoprene and terpene oxidation loads the lumped peroxy pool far more heavily, so WRF's one-shot 60 s step differs from the continuous limit by up to 3.0e-2 here (CH3OH at step 1) against 1.6e-3 on the idealized column -- a factor of 19. Convergence is first order as before (3.0e-2, 1.6e-4, 1.6e-5, 1.6e-6 over nsub = 1, 100, 1000, 10000, 100000), so the reference sits well inside rel 1e-5 of the limit. Measured: the largest relative residual over the species above 100 molec/cm3 is 6.4e-08 (LIM at t = 1.0 s). The absolute floor of 1e-9 molec/cm3 binds on nothing at all: every one of the 64 integrated species is above the floor at both output times..\n\nState: data/eqwefic/dumps/chem170_rich/esm_dump_cbmz_kpp_1.json level k = 1; reference chem170_rich/replay/kptn_1_{10,60}_100000.json; run script data/eqwefic/chem170_rich/run.sh.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0,
            "end": 1.0
          },
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 1e-05,
            "abs": 1e-09
          },
          "initial_conditions": {
